|~ `::desc`, the kind of a value turned to descend, whose rank in the
   one order is the reverse of its payload's, and the laws declared under
   it.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:desc
  |~~ A desc turns a value to descend: among values of this kind the one
      order runs in reverse, so a key of `sort`, `min` or `max` under
      `desc` ranks the greater first, whatever the kind it ranks,
      `[{:score 7} {:score 9}] | sort (get /score | desc)` answering
      `[{:score 9} {:score 7}]`. One index of a vector key descends alone,
      `sort [(get /score | desc) /name]` ranking by score
      descending, then by name.

      It is a value under its tag, `::desc#5`, which `desc` lays over
      a value and takes off a value turned, `5 | desc | desc` answering
      `5`, and `payload` opens. Two values turned compare as `sort`
      ranks them, `1 | desc | gt (2 | desc)` answering `true`, and a value
      turned beside one that is not is refused as a pair of two kinds,
      `gt (2 | desc)` turning the other first.
      ::link~(::vec/sort) ::link~(::any/gt)
      ::link~(::qlang/values)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ Any value, its rank in the one order turned around. ~~|
    | proves :holdsTheValue
        |~~ The payload is the value turned. ~~|
        ~(5 | desc | payload | eq 5)
    | proves :sortsInReverse
        |~~ Turned values sort in reverse. ~~|
        ~([2 1 3] | map (desc) | sort | map payload | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :descendsAKey
        |~~ A key of `desc` sorts in reverse. ~~|
        ~(["b" "c" "a"] | sort (desc) | eq ["c" "b" "a"]);
